\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models report high Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) on safety benchmarks, but these scores can be artifacts of action collapse rather than learned avoidance. On SafeLIBERO, an SDF-augmented Pi0 configuration achieves 91.5\% CAR with 0\% Task Success Rate (TSR); 99.6\% of its safe episodes exhibit end-effector (EE) displacement below 0.1\,mm, and 73.9\% remain below 1\,$\mu$m. The robot is frozen, not safe. In contrast, Pi0.5 achieves 74.7\%\,$\pm$\,1.5\% TSR and 26.8\%\,$\pm$\,1.6\% CAR with zero fake safe episodes, all exceeding 288\,mm displacement. We propose displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\text{f}$), which excludes episodes below a displacement threshold $\tau_d$. The bimodal displacement distribution (five orders of magnitude gap between collapsed and active episodes) makes $\tau_d$ robust across $[2, 200]$\,mm; applying CAR$_\text{f}$ collapses Pi0+A1 from 91.5\% to approximately 0\%. Beyond metric contamination, EE-only Signed Distance Field (SDF) refinement is structurally limited: three ground-truth oracle variants achieve 73.1--76.9\% CAR, all below unrefined baselines (82--83\%), because SDF gradients that repel the EE simultaneously drive arm links into obstacle volumes. Attribution experiments show that collision avoidance in fine-tuned models originates from collision-free demonstration data, not geometric supervision: LoRA-only training (CAR\,=\,54.5\%) matches LoRA+SDF at $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01\}$ ($p > 0.1$). Fine-tuning produces genuine avoidance with zero frozen episodes but incurs moderate TSR degradation of ${\sim}$21--23 percentage points (74.7\% $\to$ 51.5--54.0\%). Displacement filtering is necessary for meaningful VLA safety evaluation.
\end{abstract}
